CampusBite – Restaurant Ordering & Delivery Platform

1. Introduction

CampusBite is a full-stack restaurant ordering and delivery platform developed to make online food ordering and restaurant management easier.

The application connects three types of users: customers, restaurant owners and delivery partners. Customers can browse restaurants, explore menus, place orders and track their orders. Restaurant owners can manage their restaurants, menu items and incoming orders, while delivery partners can manage their assigned deliveries.

The main purpose of CampusBite is to provide a simple and organized platform for managing the food ordering and delivery process.

2. Problem Statement

Managing food orders manually can be time-consuming for customers and restaurant owners. Customers may face difficulties in tracking their orders, while restaurant owners need an organized way to manage menus and incoming orders.

CampusBite aims to solve these problems by providing a common digital platform where customers, restaurant owners and delivery partners can interact and manage their activities.

3. Project Objectives

The main objectives of the project are:

To develop an easy-to-use online food ordering platform.

To allow customers to browse restaurants and food menus.

To provide an option to place orders and track order status.

To help restaurant owners manage their menus and orders.

To allow delivery partners to manage assigned deliveries.

To store application information in a structured database.

To implement secure login and role-based access.

To develop the application using Docker for containerization.

4. Technologies Used

The frontend of the application is developed using React.js and CSS to create the user interface.

Python and FastAPI are used for backend development and handling application requests.

Microsoft SQL Server is used to store information related to users, restaurants, menu items, orders and other activities.

SQLAlchemy is used to connect the backend with the database, while Pydantic is used for data validation.

JWT authentication is used for user authentication and authorization. Axios is used to connect the frontend with the backend.

Docker and Docker Compose are used for containerization and running the application services.

5. System Architecture

CampusBite follows a three-layer architecture consisting of the frontend, backend and database.

The frontend is developed using React.js and provides different interfaces for customers, restaurant owners and delivery partners.

The backend is developed using FastAPI and handles application logic, authentication, restaurant operations, menu management and order processing.

The database layer uses Microsoft SQL Server to store and manage application data. SQLAlchemy is used for database operations.

The frontend communicates with the backend through API requests, and the backend retrieves or updates information in the database.

6. Main Modules

Customer Module

The customer module allows users to register, log in and access the platform.

Customers can browse restaurants, search for food options, view menus and place orders. They can also track order status, view order history, cancel eligible orders, submit reviews and raise return requests.

Customers can also manage their personal profile information.

Restaurant Owner Module

The restaurant owner module helps restaurant owners manage their restaurant operations.

Owners can manage restaurant information, add and edit menu items, update food prices and manage item availability.

They can also view incoming orders, update order statuses and manage customer return requests.

Delivery Partner Module

The delivery partner module helps delivery partners manage their assigned deliveries.

Delivery partners can view assigned orders, access order information and update delivery statuses as the delivery progresses.

7. Database Design

CampusBite uses Microsoft SQL Server as its database.

The database consists of nine main tables:

Users – Stores customer, restaurant owner and delivery partner details.

Restaurants – Stores restaurant information.

Menu_Items – Stores food item details, prices and availability.

Orders – Stores customer order information and order status.

Order_Items – Stores the individual food items included in each order.

Reviews – Stores customer ratings and feedback.

Payment_Ledger – Maintains payment-related records.

Return_Requests – Stores customer return requests and their status.

Reminders – Stores order-related reminder information.

Primary keys and foreign keys are used to establish relationships between tables and maintain data consistency.

8. Development Process

The project was developed in multiple stages.

First, the database was designed and created using Microsoft SQL Server. The required tables and relationships were established.

Next, the backend was developed using FastAPI. APIs were created for authentication, restaurant management, menu operations, orders, reviews and return requests.

The frontend was then developed using React.js and CSS. Separate dashboards and interfaces were created for the three user roles.

After frontend development, the application was connected to the backend using Axios. API integration was performed to retrieve and update application data.

Finally, Docker and Docker Compose were used to containerize and run the frontend and backend services.

9. Testing

The application was tested to check whether its main functionalities were working correctly.

Testing included user registration and login, restaurant creation, menu management, order placement, order status updates and delivery status updates.

Customer reviews, return requests and profile management were also tested.

Database connectivity and communication between the frontend and backend were checked to ensure that application data was being retrieved and updated correctly.

10. Conclusion

CampusBite demonstrates the development of a full-stack restaurant ordering and delivery platform.

The project combines frontend development, backend API development, database management, authentication and containerization into one application.

Through this project, practical knowledge was gained in React.js, Python, FastAPI, Microsoft SQL Server, SQLAlchemy, API integration and Docker.

The application provides a foundation for managing restaurant ordering and delivery activities through a centralized platform.

11. Future Enhancements

In the future, CampusBite can be improved by adding online payment gateway integration, real-time GPS-based delivery tracking, email and SMS notifications, advanced food recommendations and improved analytics dashboards.

The application can also be deployed on cloud platforms to make it accessible without depending on a local computer.

